{
  "approach_chosen": "both",
  "rationale": "Top-down uses the US field service management (FSM) software market total from published analyst reports, narrowed to the SMB scheduling-and-dispatch segment. Bottom-up counts the population of US HVAC, plumbing, and electrical service businesses with 1–100 employees, applies an estimated penetration-eligible share, and multiplies by researched ARPU benchmarks for SMB field-service SaaS. Both approaches are independently constructed; the comparison tests whether the implied customer counts are consistent with the market-size figures.",
  "metadata": {"run_id": "cw-ms-remote-0a1b2c3d"}
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim_attacked": "The 55% SMB share used to narrow the top-down TAM to SAM (the Segment % assumption), sourced to the Fieldproxy blog",
      "what_is_true": "The Fieldproxy blog post cited is a marketing publication by a competing FSM software vendor (Fieldproxy.ai). A web search found that the 58% figure from that blog describes 'HVAC businesses already using AI-powered features for scheduling and optimization' \u2014 not SMB share of FSM software spending. The 44\u201358% range the analysis attributes to this source as 'SMB share of HVAC FSM spend' does not appear to match what the blog actually measures. A competitor's unsourced blog post is not a reliable basis for this narrowing step, and the figure it contains may describe a different statistic than the analysis claims.",
      "evidence_quote": "58% of HVAC businesses already leveraging AI-powered features",
      "source_url": "https://www.fieldproxy.ai/blog/20-field-service-management-statistics-every-hvac-owner-should-know-d1-38",
      "source_title": "20 Field Service Management Statistics Every HVAC Owner Should Know (Fieldproxy blog)",
      "severity": "high",
      "quote_verified": null,
      "parameter": "segment_pct"
    },
    {
      "claim_attacked": "The US FSM software market total of $2.1 billion used as the top-down starting point",
      "what_is_true": "Multiple credible analyst firms produce US FSM software market estimates for 2024 that range from $1.3 billion (IMARC Group) to $2.6 billion (IBISWorld). The analysis uses $2.1 billion from P&S Intelligence, which sits at the upper end of this range. IMARC's figure would reduce the derived HVAC/plumbing/electrical TAM from $567M to roughly $351M \u2014 a 38% reduction. The analysis correctly notes the figure is partially supported, but it does not disclose that a major credible source is 38% lower.",
      "evidence_quote": "It puts the US market at USD 1.3 Billion in 2024, with a forecast of USD 4.6 Billion by 2033.",
      "source_url": "https://imarcgroup.com/united-states-field-service-management-market",
      "source_title": "IMARC Group \u2014 United States Field Service Management Market",
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote_verified": null,
      "parameter": "industry_total"
    },
    {
      "claim_attacked": "The claimed 2.1% convergence between top-down TAM ($567M) and bottom-up TAM ($579M) as mutual validation",
      "what_is_true": "The analysis records a 63% FSM software adoption rate among trades contractors (sourced from the Fieldproxy blog), and uses this figure only as 'context.' But the bottom-up TAM is built by multiplying ALL 193,049 employer establishments by $3,000 ARPU \u2014 treating every establishment as a current software customer. If only 63% of those establishments use FSM software, the bottom-up TAM for the current market would be approximately 193,049 \u00d7 63% \u00d7 $3,000 = ~$365M, not $579M. The top-down TAM ($567M) represents current FSM software revenue. The bottom-up TAM ($579M) represents the theoretical full-penetration revenue. These measure different things, so their 2.1% closeness does not confirm accuracy \u2014 it may be coincidental.",
      "evidence_quote": "One vendor blog (Fieldproxy) reports 63% of contractors now use some form of FSM software. Used only for context; not a direct sizing input.",
      "source_url": "internal:analysis",
      "source_title": "The source validation \u2014 figure checks, software adoption rate notes",
      "severity": "medium",
      "quote_verified": null
    },
    {
      "claim_attacked": "The FSM software adoption rate of 63% attributed as a sourced figure to the Fieldproxy blog",
      "what_is_true": "The 63% adoption rate comes from a marketing blog published by Fieldproxy, which is itself a competing FSM software vendor. Fieldproxy's blog posts do not cite original research for their statistics, as confirmed by a search that found the same blog contains multiple statistics without source attribution. A vendor's own blog, promoting adoption of the product category the vendor sells, is a conflicted source for an industry adoption rate. The analysis labels this 'sourced' with 'confidence: low,' but does not note the source conflict.",
      "evidence_quote": "The posts are useful as a starting point for vendor claims, but I wouldn't cite any of these numbers without finding the original studies. The SMB adoption figures, in particular, need independent verification. I didn't find a primary source in these results.",
      "source_url": "https://www.fieldproxy.ai/blog/20-field-service-management-statistics-every-hvac-owner-should-know-d1-38",
      "source_title": "20 Field Service Management Statistics Every HVAC Owner Should Know (Fieldproxy blog)",
      "severity": "low",
      "quote_verified": null,
      "parameter": "segment_pct"
    },
    {
      "claim_attacked": "The customer count of 193,049 as the total addressable business count, without any reduction for software non-adopters in the bottom-up TAM",
      "what_is_true": "The analysis's target segment is businesses that would pay for scheduling and dispatch software. The analysis itself notes a 63% adoption rate for FSM software in these trades. Using all 193,049 employer establishments at full ARPU for the bottom-up TAM implicitly assumes every establishment is in the market for Cadence's product. A more conservative TAM would separate current-market sizing (63% of establishments who already buy some FSM software, i.e. ~121,600 businesses, representing a switching market) from full-potential sizing (all 193,049). The analysis does not make this distinction explicit, and the 2.1% convergence with the top-down figure does not resolve it.",
      "evidence_quote": "customer_count * arpu",
      "source_url": "internal:analysis",
      "source_title": "The sizing calculation \u2014 bottom-up TAM formula",
      "severity": "low",
      "quote_verified": null,
      "parameter": "customer_count"
    }
